With Inertia SSR off, `<Head>` is only applied after the JavaScript app hydrates. So it can appear in DevTools/client-side DOM but not in “View Page Source” or the initial HTML crawlers receive.

For crawler-critical directives like `noindex`, put them somewhere server-rendered:

```blade
{{-- resources/views/app.blade.php --}}
@if($noindex ?? false)
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
@endif
```

Or, often better for `noindex`, send an HTTP header from Laravel:

```php
return Inertia::render('Page')
    ->toResponse($request)
    ->withHeaders([
        'X-Robots-Tag' => 'noindex, nofollow',
    ]);
```

Use Inertia `<Head>` for client-visible titles/meta and social tags when SSR is off, but use Blade or `X-Robots-Tag` for anything crawlers must see in the initial response. Re-enable Inertia SSR only if you need the whole page/head rendered server-side.

Most likely your `mutationFn` is not actually rejecting. React Query only calls `onError` if the mutation promise rejects. If you catch the error inside `mutationFn` and return something, React Query treats it as success, so the optimistic cache stays.

Check for this pattern:

```ts
mutationFn: async (input) => {
  try {
    return await api.updateThing(input)
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err)
    return null // this prevents onError
  }
}
```

Fix it by rethrowing:

```ts
mutationFn: async (input) => {
  try {
    return await api.updateThing(input)
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err)
    throw err
  }
}
```

And make sure your optimistic rollback context is returned from `onMutate`:

```ts
const mutation = useMutation({
  mutationFn: updateTodo,

  onMutate: async (newTodo) => {
    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })

    const previousTodos = queryClient.getQueryData(['todos'])

    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], (old) =>
      old?.map((todo) =>
        todo.id === newTodo.id ? { ...todo, ...newTodo } : todo
      )
    )

    return { previousTodos }
  },

  onError: (_error, _variables, context) => {
    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], context?.previousTodos)
  },

  onSettled: () => {
    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })
  },
})
```

Also check these common gotchas:

- `fetch()` does not reject on HTTP 400/500 by default. You must throw manually:

```ts
const res = await fetch('/api/todos', options)

if (!res.ok) {
  throw new Error('Update failed')
}

return res.json()
```

- If using Axios, do not catch and return `error.response`.
- If you pass per-call handlers to `mutate(variables, { onError })`, make sure you are not overriding behavior accidentally.
- In React Query v5, `onError` receives `context` as the third argument from `onMutate`.

So the short diagnosis: `onMutate` working but `onError` not firing almost always means the mutation is resolving instead of rejecting.

Most likely your `mutationFn` is not actually rejecting, so React Query considers the mutation successful. `onError` only fires if the mutation promise rejects or throws.

Common culprit:

```ts
mutationFn: async (vars) => {
  const res = await fetch("/api/item", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(vars) })

  // fetch does not throw on 400/500
  if (!res.ok) {
    return res.json() // this resolves, so onError will not run
  }

  return res.json()
}
```

Fix it by throwing:

```ts
mutationFn: async (vars) => {
  const res = await fetch("/api/item", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(vars) })

  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error("Mutation failed")
  }

  return res.json()
}
```

Then rollback should work like:

```ts
const mutation = useMutation({
  mutationFn: updateItem,

  onMutate: async (newItem) => {
    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ["items"] })

    const previousItems = queryClient.getQueryData(["items"])

    queryClient.setQueryData(["items"], (old: Item[] = []) =>
      old.map((item) => item.id === newItem.id ? { ...item, ...newItem } : item)
    )

    return { previousItems }
  },

  onError: (_error, _newItem, context) => {
    queryClient.setQueryData(["items"], context?.previousItems)
  },

  onSettled: () => {
    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["items"] })
  },
})
```

Also check these:

- If using Axios, make sure you are not catching and returning the error:

```ts
// Bad
try {
  return await api.post(...)
} catch (err) {
  return err
}
```

Use:

```ts
// Good
return api.post(...)
```

or:

```ts
catch (err) {
  throw err
}
```

- Make sure `onError` is inside the `useMutation({ ... })` options, not passed to `mutate` incorrectly.
- In React Query v5, `onMutate` rollback data is passed as the third argument to `onError`: `onError(error, variables, context)`.

So the short answer: your rollback is probably fine; the mutation is resolving instead of failing. Throw from `mutationFn`.
